<h1 class="chapter"><span class="num">7</span>Fixing it: robustness-preserving fine-tuning</h1>
<p>If erosion is forgetting, the obvious fix is to keep reminding the model. The project compares four
ways to do that while running exactly the same recipe, data and number of steps:</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">method</th><th>how it works</th><th style="width:18%">extra cost</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>secure format</b></td><td>put retrieved context / tool results into the <code>input</code> role when building
  the fine-tuning data</td><td>none</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>replay</b></td><td>add one hardening-style example (injected prompt, legitimate answer) to every training
  step</td><td>~1/8 more data</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>re-harden</b></td><td>after fine-tuning, run a short SecAlign DPO pass (256 pairs)</td><td>a second training stage</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>SecAnchor</b> (this project)</td><td>add SecAlign's own DPO loss on two "anchor" pairs to every step, measured
  against the hardened base, so any update that makes the injected answer relatively more likely is pushed back
  immediately</td><td>2 pairs per step (2.4× training time on the Dolly pilot)</td></tr>
</table>
