<h1 class="chapter"><span class="num">9</span>Limitations, and how to read results critically</h1>
<ul>
  <li><b>Small models.</b> 0.5B and 360M models on a CPU, hardened with a small replication of the SecAlign recipe.
    The direction of an effect is more trustworthy than its exact size; 8B+ models may behave differently.</li>
  <li><b>Short fine-tunes, one seed.</b> 96 optimizer steps per run and (mostly) one random seed per configuration.
    Real fine-tunes are longer; differences of a few points between two runs may be noise. That is why the
    project leans on paired tests rather than eyeballing percentages.</li>
  <li><b>Canary tasks are a proxy.</b> They measure whether the model obeys injected text, not whether a real
    attacker could cause damage.</li>
  <li><b>Budgeted attacks.</b> The adaptive attacks here are weak compared with ASTRA or "The Attacker Moves Second".
    Robustness numbers are an upper bound on true robustness.</li>
  <li><b>Probes are correlational</b>, measured over a handful of runs.</li>
  <li><b>Utility is measured narrowly</b> (answer accuracy on SQuAD/tool items and the recipe's held-out loss).</li>
</ul>
<div class="box key"><div class="label">Reading results like a researcher</div>
<ul>
  <li>Ask what the comparison is: here, always the same prompts before and after fine-tuning.</li>
  <li>Ask how big the uncertainty is: look at the interval, not just the point estimate.</li>
  <li>Ask what else changed: a fine-tune can raise utility and lower robustness at the same time — both matter.</li>
  <li>Ask whether a fix just moved the problem: a method that "fixes" ASR by making the model useless is not a fix.</li>
</ul></div>

