<h1 class="chapter"><span class="num">10</span>Try it yourself</h1>
<h2>Setup (about five minutes)</h2>
<pre>git clone https://github.com/shahzaib360/injdrift.git
cd injdrift
python -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate
pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest                      # ~60 offline tests on tiny random models
injdrift suites             # attacks, suites, canary tasks, profiles</pre>
<h2>Evaluate a model</h2>
<pre>injdrift eval --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct --profile smoke --fmt secure naive</pre>
<h2>Harden, fine-tune, report</h2>
<pre>injdrift harden --base Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct --n 400 \
    --out runs/qwen-secalign --results results/mine/harden.json
injdrift finetune --run-id qwen-rag --base runs/qwen-secalign --recipe rag \
    --results results/mine --cache runs/cache/qwen
injdrift report results/mine --out results/mine</pre>
<div class="box analogy"><div class="label">Engineering lesson: where did the memory go?</div>
<p>Midway through the pilot, three training runs were killed by the operating system for using too much memory,
although a LoRA run on a 0.5B model should need only 2–3 GB. Reading <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/smaps</code> showed
~4.6 GB sitting in the C library's <em>heap</em>. Each layer's activations (~1.8 MB for a 1,024-token batch) were
allocated from the heap, freed after every step, and never handed back to the operating system because the heap
had become fragmented. Two fixes, both in <code>injdrift/modeling.py</code>: allocate anything above 256 KB with
<code>mmap</code> (freed memory goes straight back), and trim the heap every few steps. Memory then stayed between
2 and 5 GB. Lesson: "the process uses 7 GB" and "the process <em>needs</em> 7 GB" are different claims — measure
before you buy a bigger machine.</p></div>

<h2>Exercises</h2>
<ol>
  <li><b>Read the attack code.</b> Open <code>src/injdrift/attacks/static.py</code>, add a new attack family (for example,
    an injection written in another language), and run the smoke profile against the undefended and hardened
    Qwen models. Which model resists it better?</li>
  <li><b>Break the sanitizer.</b> <code>formats.sanitize</code> removes control tokens until nothing changes. Write a
    test with a string that would reassemble a <code>&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code> token if the function ran only once.</li>
  <li><b>Change one factor.</b> Re-run the <code>rag</code> recipe with <code>--fmt secure</code> instead of
    <code>naive</code>. Predict the result before you look.</li>
  <li><b>Statistics.</b> Using <code>injdrift.stats.mcnemar_one_sided</code>, find the smallest number of newly broken
    prompts (with zero newly fixed) that gives p &lt; 0.05.</li>
  <li><b>Gate a model.</b> Run <code>injdrift gate</code> comparing a hardened model with one of your fine-tunes and read
    <code>gate.md</code>. Which check is closest to failing?</li>
</ol>

<!-- ============================================================== glossary -->
<h1 class="chapter">A · Glossary</h1>
<dl class="glossary">
<dt>Adaptive attack</dt><dd>An attack that changes its strategy based on the defense, e.g. trying many variants or optimizing a suffix.</dd>
<dt>ASR (attack success rate)</dt><dd>Fraction of attacked prompts where the model obeyed the injection.</dd>
<dt>Best-of-K</dt><dd>Fraction of items broken by at least one of K attack variants.</dd>
<dt>Canary task</dt><dd>A harmless injected instruction with an exact expected reply, used to detect obedience objectively.</dd>
<dt>Catastrophic forgetting</dt><dd>Losing previously learned behaviour after training on new data.</dd>
<dt>Chosen / rejected</dt><dd>The preferred and dispreferred answers in a preference (DPO) training example.</dd>
<dt>CI gate</dt><dd>An automatic check that blocks a change (here, a model) when it fails a test.</dd>
<dt>d′ (d-prime)</dt><dd>Separation between two groups in units of their spread.</dd>
<dt>DPO</dt><dd>Direct Preference Optimization: training a model to prefer chosen over rejected answers relative to a frozen reference.</dd>
<dt>Fine-tuning</dt><dd>Further training of an existing model on task-specific data.</dd>
<dt>GCG</dt><dd>Greedy Coordinate Gradient: an attack that uses gradients to search for an adversarial token suffix.</dd>
<dt>Hidden state</dt><dd>The vector of numbers a transformer layer computes for each token.</dd>
<dt>Indirect prompt injection</dt><dd>An injected instruction that arrives through data (web page, email, tool output) rather than from the user.</dd>
<dt>Input role</dt><dd>A separate chat turn for untrusted data, used by Meta SecAlign and by this project's secure format.</dd>
<dt>Linear probe</dt><dd>A simple classifier (here logistic regression) trained on hidden states to read out a property.</dd>
<dt>Log-probability</dt><dd>The logarithm of the probability a model assigns to some text; closer to 0 means more likely.</dd>
<dt>LoRA</dt><dd>Low-rank adaptation: fine-tuning small add-on matrices instead of all weights.</dd>
<dt>McNemar test</dt><dd>A test for paired yes/no outcomes that only looks at the pairs that changed.</dd>
<dt>Prompt injection</dt><dd>Text that makes a model follow instructions its operator did not intend.</dd>
<dt>Sanitization</dt><dd>Removing control tokens (chat markers) from untrusted text before building the prompt.</dd>
<dt>SFT</dt><dd>Supervised fine-tuning on example conversations.</dd>
<dt>Utility</dt><dd>How well the model still does its legitimate job.</dd>
</dl>

